#!/usr/bin/env python3

# Measure the sustained-contact level's roughness and speed exponents, which are what the contact model is
# accepted against. Run from anywhere.
#
# Usage: script/surface/ContactLadder [grid|sweep|sphere|spectrum|gradient|damping|modes|rate]
#   grid   (default) Dang 2013's own operating envelope, five roughnesses by five sliding speeds.
#          Reports the roughness exponent at each speed and the speed exponent at each roughness.
#   sweep  The roughness exponent at four sliding speeds spanning the corpus's own, for a quick check.
#   sphere The same ladder on a sphere, whose point contact takes the load-set force law instead.
#   spectrum Perret-Liaudet 2003 s5.3.3: Hertz against a linear law at the same clamp, band by band.
#          Measured: identical where the energy is, so the clamp carries the response, not the exponent.
#   gradient Ben Abdelounis 2011 Eq. (8): hold the surface gradient, step the length scale. He finds the
#          level depends on the roughness-to-spacing ratio alone, so every rung should read one level.
#   damping Perret-Liaudet 2003 Fig. 16 and Dang 2013: 10x modal damping is -10 dB, an energy balance.
#          Measured: -0.81 and -0.78 per decade against -1, so the level follows an energy balance.
#   modes  Dang 2013 Fig. 13 and Ben Abdelounis 2010: the PSD peaks sit at the resonator's own
#          eigenfrequencies, so the contact drives the modes rather than detuning them.
#   rate   The shock rate the renderer produces against sliding speed, which is acceptance invariant 1b.
#
# The acceptance test is the invariance, not the value. Dang tabulates the roughness exponent at each of his
# five speeds and gets 0.63 to 0.67, a spread of 0.04 across a 35x span in speed, and the speed exponent at
# each of his five roughnesses and gets 0.52 to 0.63. Ben Abdelounis 2010 measured the roughness exponent at
# 0.8 to 1.16 and the speed exponent at 0.7 to 0.96 experimentally, and states outright that the two are
# independent. So the level separates as Ra^n V^m, and matching one exponent at one operating point tests
# nothing. Report both spreads.
#
# Traps this harness exists to stop repeating:
#   - Bit-identical output means the bracket did not land. This grid is deterministic, so two runs of the
#     same code agree exactly, and a bracket that changes nothing looks exactly like a parameter that does
#     not matter. Both readings are the same bytes and only one is a result. The first LADDER_SEED step was
#     a part in a billion, which the loader's float32 rounded away, and four identical grids read as a
#     surface that does not matter. Before believing a null, confirm the knob reached the engine.
#   - The roughness ladder does not hold its surface. Dang's Table 8 steps the correlation length 400, 450,
#     500, 500, 500 um, and the engine hashes the realization from the correlation length and the spacing,
#     so its five rungs draw three different surfaces. Part of the roughness exponent is the surface
#     changing underneath it. The speed ladder holds one correlation length and is the cleaner of the two.
#   - The two source papers name the exponents oppositely. Dang 2013's Eq. (1) is Ra^m V^n and Ben Abdelounis
#     2011's Eq. (7) is Ra^n V^m, so a bare pair of ranges carries no axis with it. Quote every exponent with
#     the axis it belongs to.
#   - The bodies are frictionless so they hold their speed for the whole render. A body that runs off the end
#     of its lane renders as silence rather than as an error, so the lane is sized once for every rung.
#   - Sizing the lane per rung varies the geometry with the parameter and invalidates the ladder.
#   - The corpus's own SlideSpeed of 1.4 m/s is twice Dang's fastest and reads a roughness exponent near
#     zero. Measuring at the corpus's speed is worthless.
#   - Each scene is one body, since a scene is a mono mix and two bodies cannot be separated afterwards.
#   - The numbers depend on the probe. What is measured is the modal bank's output level, so a bank that
#     rings far longer than shocks arrive reads its own filter as much as the drive. No exponent from here
#     is a property of the contact model alone. Quote the probe with the exponent.
#     LADDER_T60, LADDER_HIGH and LADDER_PRESSURE were an older synthetic probe's knobs and are
#     gone: the rig is a solved bar carrying its own band and decays, and the output is pressure
#     with a per-mode gain flat across that band.
#   - The quantity matters as much as the probe. Reading the level as a velocity rather than a
#     pressure was worth about 0.2 in the roughness exponent, moving it from entirely below Dang's
#     band to bracketing it while moving the speed exponent the other way. Pressure is the default
#     because comparing a pressure against a velocity is not a comparison at all.
#   - The probe's damping law is not a lever. Constant zeta = 0.02, which is what both papers run,
#     reads roughness spread 0.322 against 0.316 and speed 0.128 against 0.108. See LADDER_ZETA.
#
# The instrument's own floor, which is what decides whether a difference is a result:
#   - The floor is the surface, not the renderer. Three runs of unchanged code give bit-identical
#     levels, exponents and spreads, so there is no run-to-run scatter at all. Stepping LADDER_SEED
#     over four realizations moves an exponent by up to 0.049 and a spread by up to 0.055, so about
#     0.04 on an exponent and 0.05 on a spread is the floor, and it is a property of the surface.
#     An unpaired difference smaller than that is not a result.
#   - The contact is sensitive, so a change that alters no physics still moves the numbers.
#     Measured two ways, swapping a divide for a tabulated reciprocal and reversing the order a
#     coupling sum accumulates in. Both are the same arithmetic to a part in ten million, and both
#     move a spread: the clamp is a hard nonlinearity, so a last-bit perturbation decides which
#     side of it a marginal cell lands on and the shock train diverges from there.
#   - That is sampling noise rather than a floor, and LADDER_PLAY is the lever. Four times the
#     render shrinks the summation-order null on the roughness spread by 2.29x, against the 2.00x
#     pure averaging predicts, and it narrows the seed spread the same way: two realizations read a
#     gap of 0.090 at 6 s and 0.022 at 24 s. So a good part of what reads as surface-to-surface
#     variability is short renders.
#   - Render longer before believing a small difference, rather than declaring a threshold. Anything
#     marginal at 6 s is re-run at 24 s or beyond. A change worth believing keeps its sign across
#     realizations and ideally survives a second implementation of itself.

import math
import os
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

Repo = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent.parent
Out = Path(os.environ.get("TMPDIR", "/tmp")) / "contact_ladder"
sys.path.insert(0, str(Repo / "glTF_PhysicalAudio/samples"))
import generate as gen

# Seconds rendered per rung. Longer averages more shocks into every level, which is the lever on how finely
# two code states can be told apart: this contact is sensitive enough that a change altering no physics
# still moves a level, and only averaging shrinks that. Measured: a null change moves the roughness spread
# 0.027 at 6 s and 0.012 at 24 s, so the noise falls as the square root of the length. 12 s puts it near
# 0.017, which resolves anything worth 0.05 at three to one, and that covers every question this ladder has
# open. Going longer buys 0.012, which settles nothing 12 s does not.
# LADDER_PLAY=6 to iterate. Longer only for a specific marginal question, and say which.
Play = float(os.environ.get("LADDER_PLAY", "12.0"))
Settle = 2.0  # seconds dropped before the level is read, so the landing transient is gone
LaneHalf = 80.0  # metres, long enough that the fastest rung cannot run off it
# Modal damping ratio, applied to every mode alike, which is what both papers this ladder is judged against
# use: Dang 2013 and Ben Abdelounis 2011 each run zeta = 0.02 on all modes. Zero keeps the solved bar's own
# Rayleigh decays.
# Measured 2026-08-03 on an earlier probe, and it was not the lever. At zeta = 0.02 the roughness
# spread read 0.322 against the default 0.316 and the speed spread 0.128 against 0.108. Kept as a bracket.
ProbeZeta = float(os.environ.get("LADDER_ZETA", "0"))
# Which surface realization every rung draws. The engine seeds a track's phases from a hash of the
# correlation length, the spectral slope and the sample spacing, and roughness is not among them, so a rung's
# realization is fixed unless one of those three moves. Stepping the slope redraws the surface while leaving
# the spectrum and the Hurst exponent alone to six figures, which is the only handle on the realization from
# outside the engine.
# The step is a part in a million because the slope reaches the engine as a float32, whose spacing near 2.4 is
# about 3e-7. A finer step survives the glTF, which carries full precision, and is then rounded away on load,
# so it reads as four identical runs and looks like a surface that does not matter.
# Every exponent here is measured on one surface. This is what says whether a small difference between two
# code states is the model or is this surface.
LadderSeed = int(os.environ.get("LADDER_SEED", "0"))
# The level is read as the renderer's own far-field pressure. Dang and Ben Abdelounis report vibrational
# velocity (each referred to 1e-9 m/s), and the radiation corner ka = 1 sits near 1.1 kHz at the slider's
# size, below the solved bar's 1.8 kHz first mode, so over the probe's band each mode's pressure is its
# velocity times a constant and pressure levels carry the same spreads and exponents.


# The rig's own bar, longer and thinner than the corpus's so its solved band covers the shock rates the
# slow rungs actually drive. Solved f1 is 52.4 Hz (70 Hz at the 0.5 kg slider's scale) where the corpus
# bar's is 1.8 kHz, and 40 modes reach 7.6 kHz solved, matching Dang's 40-mode resonator over 29 Hz to
# 10 kHz. Every ladder number recorded before this bar landed (2026-08-05) rode a rig whose lowest mode
# sat above every shock rate below 0.2 m/s.
RigBarHalf = (0.45, 0.03, 0.004)
# Every rung's body. The engine sizes the body on the solved model as a scale change,
# mass = solved mass * s^3, and retunes its frequencies by 1/s.
SliderMass = 0.5  # kg


def ladder_probe(zeta=None):
    """The rig's probe: the rig bar solved by the real chain, with its decays replaced by a constant
    damping ratio when one is asked for. `zeta` overrides LADDER_ZETA, so a sweep can step it per rung."""
    solved = dict(gen.solved_modes(RigBarHalf, gen.CERAMIC, modes=40))
    z = ProbeZeta if zeta is None else zeta
    if z > 0:
        solved["decayRates"] = [z * 2 * math.pi * f for f in solved["frequencies"]]
    return solved

# Dang 2013 Table 8: quadratic roughness against correlation length. His correlation length barely moves
# while Ra spans eleven-fold, so his roughness ladder varies the surface gradient, as this one does.
Profiles = [(3.57e-6, 400e-6), (6.02e-6, 450e-6), (11.88e-6, 500e-6), (25.80e-6, 500e-6), (38.61e-6, 500e-6)]
Speeds = [0.02, 0.04, 0.1, 0.2, 0.7]  # m/s, his seven collapsed to five
# Dang's own speeds are the default. Override to resolve a feature between them, which is how a narrow
# artifact is told from a broad one: LADDER_SPEEDS=0.1,0.14,0.2,0.28,0.4
if os.environ.get("LADDER_SPEEDS"):
    Speeds = [float(s) for s in os.environ["LADDER_SPEEDS"].split(",")]
DangRoughness = [0.63, 0.64, 0.66, 0.65, 0.67]  # his Fig. 15, one per speed
DangSpeed = [0.52, 0.63, 0.58, 0.55, 0.54]  # his Fig. 14, one per roughness


def build(name, roughness, correlation_length, speed, shape="box", zeta=None):
    """One frictionless body sliding on one finish, varying a single parameter.
    A box lands on its face and takes the geometry-set force law, and a sphere on its point takes Hertz."""
    gen.OUT = Out
    # Waviness is carried with the finish, as the corpus carries it. Dang's own profiles are synthesized
    # flat above their finish, but his slider is 20 mm where this body is a 9 cm face, and under the
    # statistical bed a face that wide with nothing holding it apart bears on more asperities than can
    # turn over and renders silent. LADDER_WAVINESS scales it: 0 is Dang's own flat-above-finish class,
    # which the element container renders fine (footprint-window turnover needs no mesoscale), and it
    # removes the per-rung waviness realization lottery. The lottery is structural on this ladder: the
    # waviness length rides 40 lc while lc changes across the roughness rungs, so each rung redraws its
    # ~4-period-per-footprint waviness field, and measured 2026-08-10 (24 s, four seeds, springs on) that
    # draw is worth a factor of two on the roughness exponent (seed means 0.32 0.31 0.69 0.69).
    # Dang's idealised geometry is covered by the shock-distribution comparison in ContactModelTest instead.
    waviness_scale = float(os.environ.get("LADDER_WAVINESS", "1"))
    surface = {"name": "Ladder", "material": 0, "roughness": roughness,
               "correlationLength": correlation_length,
               "spectralSlope": gen.FINISHES["Machined"]["spectralSlope"] * (1 + LadderSeed * 1e-6),
               "waviness": waviness_scale * roughness, "wavinessLength": 40 * correlation_length}
    gen.scene(
        name, "ladder rung",
        bodies=[gen.body("Slider", SliderMass, 1, 0, shape)],
        grounds=[gen.ground("Ladder", 0, 1)],
        surfaces=[surface],
        physics_materials=[{"staticFriction": 0.0, "dynamicFriction": 0.0, "restitution": 0.0}],
        probe=ladder_probe(zeta),
        speed=speed, lane_half=(LaneHalf, 0.04, 0.12), start_x=-0.9 * LaneHalf,
        bar_half=RigBarHalf,
    )
    return Out / f"{name}.gltf"


# Rungs rendered at once. Each is an independent process and none has to wait for another.
# Measured 2026-08-04 on the 6 s grid: 50 s at one job, 20 at four, 17 at eight, 29 at sixteen. Renders are
# GPU-paced and contend, so eight is the floor and past it they queue on the GPU and lose. The full 12 s
# grid runs in about 26 s, against five minutes for the serial 24 s one it replaces.
# Rendering in parallel does not change a single number: one job and eight give bit-identical grids.
Jobs = int(os.environ.get("LADDER_JOBS", "8"))


def render_many(paths):
    """Every rung's samples, rendered several at a time and returned in the order asked for."""
    from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor
    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=Jobs) as pool:
        return list(pool.map(render, paths))


def decode(recording):
    """The mono samples of a recorded render."""
    p = subprocess.run(["ffmpeg", "-v", "error", "-i", str(recording), "-map", "0:a", "-f", "f32le",
                        "-ac", "1", "-ar", "48000", "-"], capture_output=True)
    if p.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(f"decode {recording.name} exited {p.returncode}")
    import array
    a = array.array("f")
    a.frombytes(p.stdout)
    return a


def render(path):
    """Render one rung headless and return its mono samples."""
    wav = path.with_suffix(".wav")
    wav.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    r = subprocess.run([str(Repo / "build/MeshEditor"), str(path), "--headless", "--play", str(Play),
                        "--timeline-end", str(Play + 1), "--record", str(wav)], capture_output=True)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(f"render {path.name} exited {r.returncode}: {r.stderr[-400:]!r}")
    a = decode(wav)
    return a


def level(samples):
    """Mean power after the settle, which is Dang's own gauge: he reports time-averaged (Leq)
    vibration levels. The footprint contact is a sparse shot process at slow speeds (bearing
    elements cross the window edge a few times a second), and a median frame sits in the silence
    between shots, reading the between-events floor while the shots carry the roughness law.
    Measured 2026-08-10 on the seed-0 grid: the v=0.02 roughness exponent reads -0.44 on the
    median and the v=0.1 row +0.33 on the mean from the same wavs. Every exponent recorded
    before 2026-08-10 is a median-frame level and does not compare against readings from here."""
    start = int(Settle * 48000)
    if len(samples) <= start:
        return 0.0
    return sum(s * s for s in samples[start:]) / (len(samples) - start)


def exponent(xs, levels):
    """Least-squares slope of log(level) against log(x), halved because a level is a power."""
    pts = [(math.log(x), math.log(y)) for x, y in zip(xs, levels) if y > 0]
    if len(pts) < 2:
        return float("nan")
    mx = sum(p[0] for p in pts) / len(pts)
    my = sum(p[1] for p in pts) / len(pts)
    den = sum((p[0] - mx) ** 2 for p in pts)
    return sum((p[0] - mx) * (p[1] - my) for p in pts) / den / 2


def spread(values):
    return max(values) - min(values)


def grid():
    # Every rung is built first and then rendered together, so the grid waits once rather than 25 times.
    rungs = [(si, ri, build(f"g{si}_{ri}", rq, lc, v))
             for si, v in enumerate(Speeds) for ri, (rq, lc) in enumerate(Profiles)]
    levels = {(si, ri): level(s) for (si, ri, _), s in zip(rungs, render_many([r[2] for r in rungs]))}
    for si, v in enumerate(Speeds):
        print(f"  v={v}: " + " ".join(f"{levels[(si, ri)]:.3e}" for ri in range(len(Profiles))), flush=True)

    rq = [p[0] for p in Profiles]
    rough = [exponent(rq, [levels[(si, ri)] for ri in range(len(Profiles))]) for si in range(len(Speeds))]
    speed = [exponent(Speeds, [levels[(si, ri)] for si in range(len(Speeds))]) for ri in range(len(Profiles))]
    print("\nroughness exponent at each speed")
    for v, m, d in zip(Speeds, rough, DangRoughness):
        print(f"  v={v:<5} m/s   {m:+.3f}   (Dang {d})")
    print("speed exponent at each roughness")
    for (r, _), n, d in zip(Profiles, speed, DangSpeed):
        print(f"  Rq={r * 1e6:<5.1f} um  {n:+.3f}   (Dang {d})")
    print(f"\nSPREAD roughness {spread(rough):.3f} (Dang {spread(DangRoughness):.2f})"
          f"  speed {spread(speed):.3f} (Dang {spread(DangSpeed):.2f})")


def sweep():
    """The roughness exponent against sliding speed, which is where its non-invariance shows up fastest."""
    rq = [p[0] for p in Profiles]
    speeds = (0.02, 0.1, 0.4, 1.4)
    samples = render_many([build(f"s{v}_{ri}", r, lc, v) for v in speeds for ri, (r, lc) in enumerate(Profiles)])
    for i, v in enumerate(speeds):
        levels = [level(s) for s in samples[i * len(Profiles):(i + 1) * len(Profiles)]]
        print(f"  v={v:<5} m/s   roughness exponent {exponent(rq, levels):+.3f}", flush=True)


def gradient():
    """Hold the surface gradient and step the length scale, on the ShockRate scene's own three surfaces,
    whose ratio is held at 0.04 by construction.

    Ben Abdelounis 2011's Eq. (8) has the level collapsing onto the roughness-to-spacing ratio alone,
    independent of sliding speed, which would make every rung here read one level. Treat that as a weak
    prediction: his surfaces are a one-parameter family with Rsm rising with Ra, 92 to 516 um over Ra 1 to
    26 um, so Ra and Rz/Rsm are both monotone along his single ladder and his collapse cannot separate them.
    He never held the ratio and stepped the scale, which is what this does.

    Measured 2026-08-03, and the level is strongly scale dependent: 6.0 dB across the three lanes at
    0.1 m/s and 15.8 dB at 0.7 m/s, coarse loudest in both. Not the probe's band, though the rates run to
    14 kHz at 0.7 m/s: widening the probe to 20 kHz reads 6.0 and 15.8, against 6.6 and 15.6 at 7 kHz.
    So the ShockRate scene's lanes are not level matched, and a listener hears loudness across them as well
    as the grain-to-hiss character they are authored to carry."""
    scales = [1e-3, 2e-4, 5e-5]  # correlation length, m
    speeds = (0.1, 0.7)
    samples = render_many([build(f"q{v}_{i}", 0.04 * lc, lc, v) for v in speeds for i, lc in enumerate(scales)])
    for si, v in enumerate(speeds):
        levels = [level(s) for s in samples[si * len(scales):(si + 1) * len(scales)]]
        ratio = max(levels) / min(levels) if min(levels) > 0 else float("inf")
        print(f"  v={v:<5} m/s   levels " + " ".join(f"{x:.3e}" for x in levels)
              + f"   spread {10 * math.log10(ratio):.1f} dB", flush=True)


def spectrum():
    """Perret-Liaudet and Rigaud 2003 section 5.3.3, run on our model. They replace Hertz with a linear law
    carrying the same contact-loss clamp and find the response identical around the primary peak, differing
    only at high frequency, concluding the clamp is the strong nonlinearity and the exponent a correction.
    The LINEAR_SPOT bracket retired with the previous engine (2026-08-08): the quadratised exchange
    renders the Hertz spot law only, so this mode now renders one arm and the recorded comparison below
    stands as the settled measurement. A future linear bracket needs a linear law in the sampled bed.

    Measured 2026-08-03, and our model reproduces theirs. Hertz against linear, in dB per octave band:
      v=0.7 m/s  125-250 -0.15, 250-500 -0.07, 500-1k +0.69, 1k-2k -0.05, 2k-4k +0.42, 4k-8k -0.59
      v=0.1 m/s  125-250 -0.87, 250-500 -0.14, 500-1k +0.25, 1k-2k +1.18, 2k-4k +1.66, 4k-8k +2.94
    Identical where the energy is and diverging only high up, which is their finding exactly.
    So the clamp carries the response and the spot exponent is a high-frequency correction. Nothing that
    changes the exponent will move the level much, and the remaining gaps are about how load distributes
    across the cells, not about the law one spot takes.

    This mode is deterministic too, bit-identical across runs of unchanged code, measured 2026-08-04. The
    +2.94, -1.55 and -3.64 that 4k-8k at 0.1 m/s has read are three different code states, not one band
    flapping between runs. Read the bands as a shape all the same, since that band sits about 19 dB below
    the one carrying the energy and a difference there moves nothing audible."""
    import numpy as np
    for v in (0.1, 0.7):
        samples = np.asarray(render(build(f"sp{v}", 6.02e-6, 4.5e-4, v)), dtype=np.float64)
        samples = samples[int(Settle * 48000):]
        n = 1 << 14
        trimmed = samples[:len(samples) // n * n].reshape(-1, n)
        psd = (np.abs(np.fft.rfft(trimmed * np.hanning(n), axis=1)) ** 2).mean(axis=0)
        freq = np.fft.rfftfreq(n, 1 / 48000)
        print(f"  v={v} m/s", flush=True)
        for lo in (125, 250, 500, 1000, 2000, 4000, 8000):
            band = (freq >= lo) & (freq < 2 * lo)
            print(f"    {lo:>5}-{2 * lo:<5} Hz  {10 * math.log10(psd[band].sum() + 1e-300):8.2f} dB", flush=True)


def rate():
    """The shock rate the renderer produces, against sliding speed, which is acceptance invariant 1b.

    The offline sweep in ContactModelTest cannot answer this. It has no body, so nothing ever leaves the
    surface and its rate is the sliding speed over the correlation length by construction, which is exactly
    linear and tests nothing. Ben Abdelounis 2011 Figs. 6 and 7 measure the rate falling with speed, and his
    falling rate comes with a body that leaves the surface.

    CONTACT_REPORT counts, inside the renderer, a cell taking up load again after letting go as one shock,
    and the samples where the whole contact carries nothing as time the body spends clear. So the same run
    gives the rate, the flight fraction that acceptance criterion 5 wants, and the fraction of cells bearing.

    Measured 2026-08-04 at Rq 6.02 um:

        speed   shocks/s   free    bearing   level       intensity
        0.02    1718       0.121   0.098     1.459e-14   8.493e-18
        0.04    2124       0.450   0.064     4.508e-14   2.123e-17
        0.1     1984       0.736   0.036     1.358e-13   6.848e-17
        0.2     4739       0.635   0.055     1.058e-13   2.233e-17
        0.7     4887       0.846   0.029     2.037e-13   4.170e-17

    Intensity rises with speed, as speed^+0.364, which is Ben Abdelounis's sign. The offline sweep has it
    falling 38x over this range and that reading is an artifact of having no body, exactly as its rate was.
    Decomposition check: rate +0.323 and intensity +0.364 sum to the level's +0.687, so the three are one
    consistent set rather than three separate fits.
    Intensity is not monotone though: it dips at 0.2 m/s along with the level, so the fit runs through a
    feature rather than a clean law.

    The rate is sublinear in speed, going as speed^0.647 across Dang's five. Over a 35x span it rises 2.8x
    where a bodyless contact would rise 35x, so the body leaving the surface breaks the linearity outright
    and invariant 1b passes.

    A single exponent hides two regimes, and resolving between Dang's speeds with LADDER_SPEEDS shows them:

        speed   0.1    0.14   0.2    0.28   0.4    0.55   0.7
        rate    1984   3718   4739   4361   4328   4463   4887
        free    0.736  0.647  0.635  0.734  0.794  0.833  0.846

        0.10 to 0.20 m/s : rate ~ v^+1.25, superlinear
        0.20 to 0.70 m/s : rate ~ v^+0.02, flat

    So above about 0.2 m/s the shock rate stops answering to speed at all, while the flight fraction rises
    monotonically, 0.635 to 0.846. That is Ben Abdelounis's mechanism running: the body rides clear more of
    the time and the rate stops growing. It saturates rather than reversing, so the model reaches flat where
    he measures falling, which is a smaller disagreement than the whole-range fit suggests.
    Quote the regime with the exponent. The 0.647 is an average of a superlinear half and a flat one and
    describes neither.

    The rate is per cell and this contact carries up to MaxBedSpots of them at 48 kHz, so it is not comparable
    to the offline sweep's per-contact rate or to Dang's per-node one. Only its scaling is.

    Flight fraction runs 12 to 85 percent and is not monotone in speed, dipping at 0.2 m/s, which is the same
    speed at which the grid's two smoothest profiles dip in level. Whether those are one effect is unknown.
    """
    print(f"  {'speed':>6} {'shocks/s':>12} {'free':>8} {'bearing':>8} {'level':>12} {'intensity':>12}")
    rows = []
    for v in Speeds:
        path = build(f"rt{v}", 6.02e-6, 4.5e-4, v)
        wav = path.with_suffix(".wav")
        wav.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        log = Out / f"rt{v}.err"
        with open(log, "w") as err:
            # Straight to a file: the probe writes its report through this channel and a pipe loses it.
            subprocess.run([str(Repo / "build/MeshEditor"), str(path), "--headless", "--play", str(Play),
                            "--timeline-end", str(Play + 1), "--record", str(wav)],
                           stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=err, env={**os.environ, "CONTACT_REPORT": "1"})
        line = next((l for l in reversed(log.read_text().splitlines()) if l.startswith("CONTACT ")), None)
        if line is None:
            print(f"  {v:>6} no report")
            continue
        f = dict(p.split("=", 1) for p in line.split()[1:] if "=" in p)
        # The level of the same render, so rate and intensity come from one run rather than two.
        # Ben Abdelounis 2011 resolves the level into rate times intensity, so intensity is level over rate.
        lvl = level(decode(wav))
        rate_v = float(f["rate"])
        rows.append((v, rate_v, float(f["free"]), float(f["bearing"]), lvl, lvl / rate_v if rate_v > 0 else 0.0))
        print(f"  {v:>6} {rows[-1][1]:>12.4g} {rows[-1][2]:>8.4f} {rows[-1][3]:>8.4f}"
              f" {lvl:>12.4g} {rows[-1][5]:>12.4g}", flush=True)
    if len(rows) > 1:
        speeds = [r[0] for r in rows]
        # `exponent` halves its slope for an amplitude. These are powers, so undo that.
        rate_slope = 2 * exponent(speeds, [r[1] for r in rows])
        intensity_slope = 2 * exponent(speeds, [r[5] for r in rows])
        level_slope = 2 * exponent(speeds, [r[4] for r in rows])
        print(f"\n  rate      goes as speed^{rate_slope:+.3f}   (bodyless sweep gives +1.000 by construction,"
              f" Ben Abdelounis measures it NEGATIVE)")
        print(f"  intensity goes as speed^{intensity_slope:+.3f}   (Ben Abdelounis measures it POSITIVE)")
        # These three are powers, so the level's slope is the sum of the other two and is not the exponent
        # Dang tabulates. His is an amplitude, being 20log10 of a velocity, so halve this to compare.
        print(f"  level     goes as speed^{level_slope:+.3f}   = their sum. As an amplitude, which is the"
              f" convention Dang and Ben Abdelounis tabulate, that is {level_slope / 2:+.3f}.")


def modes():
    """Dang 2013 Fig. 13: the resonator's PSD peaks sit at its own eigenfrequencies, from which he concludes
    the shocks excite the resonator rather than coupling to it, and that the coupling between the two
    profiles is weak. Ben Abdelounis et al. 2010 report the same experimentally, that under light load the
    contact does not modify the natural frequencies of the sliding solids.

    Ours should do the same. A peak that sits off its mode says the contact is detuning the modes, which
    would mean the bed's stiffness is loading the resonator rather than driving it.

    Differential, and the strongest class this project has: the comparison is our peaks against our own
    modes, so neither paper's configuration enters and no absolute frequency of theirs is used. It is also
    the only check here with two independent sources behind it, one numerical and one experimental.

    A peak is located by parabolic interpolation on the log PSD across the bin and its two neighbours, which
    resolves well inside one bin, and is searched within 2% of the mode so a neighbouring mode cannot be
    picked up. Offsets are reported in cents, where 17 cents is 1%.

    The references must carry the slider's retune. The slider is sized on the solved model and the engine
    retunes its frequencies by 1/s, 490 cents at 0.5 kg on the rig bar. Comparing against the solved
    frequencies raw puts every mode outside its own 2% search window, and the search returns noise bumps
    pinned at the window edge: median 24 to 27 cents with worsts at +-35 to 37, which is the window's own
    34-cent half-width and not a detuning. The 2026-08-04 pass below happened to read correctly only
    because scale retuning was inert that day (fixed in e0311343).

    Measured 2026-08-05 on the band-matched rig with retuned references, and it passes. Median offset
    2.7, 1.6 and 0.9 cents:

        v=0.1 Rq=6.0 um   median 2.7   worst -35.0   within one bin 13/39
        v=0.7 Rq=6.0 um   median 1.6   worst -34.5   within one bin 12/39
        v=0.7 Rq=38.6 um  median 0.9   worst +34.7   within one bin 20/39

    The worsts still sit at the window edge, which is a weakly driven mode handing the search a noise bump,
    not a mode that moved: half the modes sit inside their own 1.46 Hz bin.

    Measured 2026-08-04 on the corpus bar, medians 2.1, 3.6 and 2.2 cents, the same verdict.

    The control is the roughness axis, and it is what makes this a result rather than a small number.
    Detuning would grow with the bed's stiffness. Stepping Rq 6.0 to 38.6 um at one speed, a sixfold change
    in roughness, the median offset falls, 3.6 to 2.2. The offsets also change sign between configurations.
    Unsystematic in sign and not growing with stiffness is noise, not detuning, which is Dang's finding."""
    import numpy as np
    solved = ladder_probe()
    # The rendered slider is sized on the solved model, so its modes ring at the retuned frequencies.
    slider_scale = (SliderMass / solved["mass"]) ** (1.0 / 3.0)
    freqs = [f / slider_scale for f in solved["frequencies"]]
    for v, rq in ((0.1, 6.02e-6), (0.7, 6.02e-6), (0.7, 38.61e-6)):
        samples = np.asarray(render(build(f"md{v}_{rq:.0e}", rq, 4.5e-4, v)), dtype=np.float64)
        samples = samples[int(Settle * 48000):]
        n = 1 << 15
        trimmed = samples[:len(samples) // n * n].reshape(-1, n)
        psd = (np.abs(np.fft.rfft(trimmed * np.hanning(n), axis=1)) ** 2).mean(axis=0)
        freq = np.fft.rfftfreq(n, 1 / 48000)
        bin_hz = 48000 / n
        offsets = []
        for f0 in freqs:
            band = np.flatnonzero((freq >= f0 * 0.98) & (freq <= f0 * 1.02))
            if len(band) < 3:
                continue
            i = band[int(np.argmax(psd[band]))]
            if i == 0 or i + 1 >= len(psd) or psd[i] <= 0:
                continue
            # Parabolic vertex through the peak bin and its neighbours, in log magnitude.
            y0, y1, y2 = (math.log(max(psd[j], 1e-300)) for j in (i - 1, i, i + 1))
            denom = y0 - 2 * y1 + y2
            delta = 0.5 * (y0 - y2) / denom if denom != 0 else 0.0
            f_peak = freq[i] + max(min(delta, 1.0), -1.0) * bin_hz
            offsets.append(1200 * math.log2(f_peak / f0))
        # The median, not the rms. A mode's bin is 14 cents wide at the bottom of the probe's band and 0.4 at
        # the top, so the low modes carry an offset the analysis cannot resolve away, and one weakly driven
        # mode can hand the search a noise bump instead of itself. Both inflate an rms and neither is
        # detuning. Report the median, and how many modes sit within their own bin.
        ranked = sorted(abs(o) for o in offsets)
        median = ranked[len(ranked) // 2]
        within = sum(1 for f0, o in zip(freqs, offsets) if abs(o) <= 1200 * math.log2((f0 + bin_hz) / f0))
        print(f"  v={v:<5} Rq={rq * 1e6:<5.1f} um  median {median:5.1f} cents  worst {max(offsets, key=abs):+7.1f}  "
              f"within one bin {within}/{len(offsets)}  (bin {bin_hz:.2f} Hz, 17 cents = 1%)", flush=True)


def damping():
    """Step the probe's modal damping ratio at a held operating point and fit the level's slope against it.

    Perret-Liaudet and Rigaud 2003 Fig. 16 and Dang 2013 both report 10x modal damping as -10 dB, which is an
    energy balance: the drive injects a power the resonator dissipates, so the level goes as Lv ~ zeta^-1/2 and
    the power this harness reads goes as zeta^-1, a slope of -1 per decade.

    Our model against our model, so neither paper's absolute level enters and their configuration cannot
    disagree with ours. A slope near -1 says the level is set by an energy balance. A flat one says the drive
    depends on the resonator, and one steeper than -1 says the contact feeds back on its own damping.

    Measured: -0.81 at 0.1 m/s and -0.78 at 0.7 m/s, agreeing across a 7x span in speed, so the level follows
    an energy balance about 20% short of a clean one. The shortfall is the modal deflection feeding back
    through the approach in StepVoice.
    Read the per-step dB alongside the slope. The steps run -5.9, -3.4, -5.7 at 0.1 m/s against -6.02 for each
    4x step, the same shape at both speeds. At zeta = 0.005 the probe's lowest mode has a T60 of 1.2 s against
    this harness's 0.1 s level frame, so that rung reads the bank's own integration.
    """
    rq, lc = Profiles[1] # Dang's second profile, mid-ladder
    zetas = [0.005, 0.02, 0.08, 0.32] # a 64x span, four rungs of nothing else changing
    speeds = (0.1, 0.7)
    samples = render_many([build(f"d{v}_{i}", rq, lc, v, zeta=z) for v in speeds for i, z in enumerate(zetas)])
    for si, v in enumerate(speeds):
        levels = [level(s) for s in samples[si * len(zetas):(si + 1) * len(zetas)]]
        # The power's slope against zeta, which the energy balance puts at -1. `exponent` halves it for an
        # amplitude, so undo that here: what is predicted is a power law, not an amplitude one.
        slope = 2 * exponent(zetas, levels)
        rel = " ".join(f"{10 * math.log10(x / levels[0]):+.1f}" for x in levels)
        print(f"  v={v:<5} m/s   slope {slope:+.3f} /decade (energy balance -1.000)   dB re first: {rel}", flush=True)


def sphere():
    """The same roughness ladder on a point contact, which takes the load-set force law instead.
    A level that does not answer to roughness here is the sphere's floor."""
    rq = [p[0] for p in Profiles]
    speeds = (0.02, 0.1, 0.7)
    samples = render_many([build(f"p{v}_{ri}", r, lc, v, "sphere")
                           for v in speeds for ri, (r, lc) in enumerate(Profiles)])
    for i, v in enumerate(speeds):
        levels = [level(s) for s in samples[i * len(Profiles):(i + 1) * len(Profiles)]]
        print(f"  v={v:<5} m/s   roughness exponent {exponent(rq, levels):+.3f}"
              f"   levels " + " ".join(f"{x:.2e}" for x in levels), flush=True)


